const body = `<h1>6.5c — Wire Inventory Into Your RPG</h1>

<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">RPG Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>You've built Item.java and FixedInventory.java in isolation. Now you integrate
    them into DungeonCrawl so loot from combat actually goes somewhere the player
    can use. This is the payoff: two files you built from scratch now power a real
    game feature.</p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Add Inventory to DungeonCrawl</h3>
        <p>Add a <code>FixedInventory</code> field to DungeonCrawl:</p>
        <pre><code>FixedInventory inventory = new FixedInventory(20);</code></pre>

        <p>After each combat victory, loot the monster:</p>
        <pre><code>inventory.add(new Item("Goblin Sword", 8, 25, "Common"));
inventory.add(new Item("Gold Coins", 1, 10, "Common"));</code></pre>

        <p>Add a "show inventory" command to your game loop. When the player types
        "inv", call <code>inventory.display()</code>. Show total gold value too.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Use Items in Combat</h3>
        <p>Before combat, let the player type "use Health Potion" to consume an item:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Check <code>inventory.contains("Health Potion")</code></li>
            <li>If found, call <code>inventory.remove("Health Potion")</code></li>
            <li>Call <code>player.heal(15)</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>Add a "Strength Potion" that gives +3 attack for one combat. Where do you
        store "temporary buffs" — in the item, the player, or somewhere else?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>FixedInventory has a capacity of 20. What happens when the 21st item
                drops? Is there a better way to handle this than silently losing items?</li>
            <li>You built Item as immutable. When a player "uses" a Health Potion,
                you don't change the potion — you remove it and call player.heal().
                Why is this design cleaner than making Item mutable?</li>
            <li>Look at your DungeonCrawl main loop. How many features does it
                handle now: movement, combat, inventory, item usage. Is this getting
                unwieldy? What would you do to organize this in a bigger game?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Wire a custom data structure into an existing game loop</li>
            <li>Connect inventory operations (add/remove/contains) to game events</li>
            <li>Explain the trade-off between mutable and immutable item design</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/inventory-builder.html">Inventory Builder (Student RPG Guide)</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/arrays-of-objects.html">Arrays of Objects</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/array-algorithms.html">Array Algorithms</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
